  "summary": "Three Rentals, all holding the whole image, all at one price, and one Run that reads a 40GB dataset. What separates them is what each of them can say about the path to the object store the dataset is durable in: one measured 4 Gbps, one measured 200 Mbps, and one has never measured that path at all, so the same forty gigabytes are eighty seconds of reading on the first, twenty-six minutes on the second, and Mercator's own stated prior on the third. The slow machine is the faster one everywhere else, and says so: it published a 10 Gbps path to the registry, which buys it nothing, because it holds the image already and there is nothing to fetch over that link. A flat constant per scope cannot tell these three machines apart at all, which is what makes this the fixture that says a transfer is priced from the path it crosses: restore the constant and all three price the read at 640 seconds, the placement falls to whichever offer ID sorts first, and the machine that measured a gigabit path wins nothing by having measured it. The unmeasured machine is the fallback half of the same claim, and it is priced rather than refused: silence about a path is doubt and never infeasibility, so it is charged the prior, its estimate is capped at what a guess is worth, and its record names the assumption instead of pretending a measurement. The declaration is single: the world moves the bytes at the rate the path states and the machine publishes that same rate to Mercator, so one statement cannot mean two transfer models.",
